00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
It begins.

2
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:38,000
High School Musical is a comedy, drama, family film directed by Kenny Ortega.

3
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
A popular high school athlete and an academically gifted girl get roles in the school
musical and develop a friendship that threatens East High's social order.

4
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:10,000
Troy Bolton, played by Zac Efron.

5
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:26,000
Gabriella Montez, played by Vanessa Hudgens.

6
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Sharpay Evans, played by Ashley French.

7
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:58,000
Ryan Evans, played by Lucas Grabeel.

8
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Chad Danforth, played by Corbin Bleu.

9
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:30,000
Taylor McKessie, played by Monique Coleman.

10
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
High School Musical is a 2006 American musical television film produced by and aired on
Disney Channel as part of the network's slate of original television films.

11
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:02,000
The first installment of the High School Musical series, the film was directed by
choreographer and filmmaker Kenny Ortega from a screenplay by Peter Barsocchini.

12
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:18,000
It stars Zac Efron, Vanessa Hudgens, Ashley Tisdale, Lucas
Grabeel, Alyson Reed, Corbin Bleu, and Monique Coleman.

13
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:34,000
High School Musical follows student Troy Bolton (Efron), the captain of his school basketball team, and Gabriella Montez (Hudgens), an academically
gifted transfer student, who together audition for the lead roles in their school musical, causing division among the school's cliques.

14
00:45:44,000 --> 00:45:50,000
Development for the film began after Barsocchini approached the
network in 2004 with a script idea for a musical film.

15
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:06,000
Disney executives also wanted to replicate the success of the musical television film The Cheetah Girls (2003), as well as the
standalone musical episodes of their television series Even Stevens (2000–2003) and That's So Raven (2003–2007).

16
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:22,000
Principal photography for High School Musical primarily took place in Utah, with filming
locations including East High School, Murray High School, and Salt Lake City.

17
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:38,000
Additional filming took place in Los Angeles.

18
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:54,000
The film has been described by Barsocchini and numerous
critics as a modern adaptation of Romeo & Juliet.

19
01:02:04,000 --> 01:02:10,000
High School Musical premiered on Disney Channel on January 20, 2006.

20
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:26,000
It became the most commercially successful Disney Channel television film.

21
01:08:36,000 --> 01:08:42,000
In the U.S., High School Musical generated 7.7 million viewers in its premiere
broadcast, breaking the then-record for the highest premiere for the network.

22
01:11:52,000 --> 01:11:58,000
Internationally, the film also saw considerable success; as of 2019, over 225
million unique viewers were calculated as watched High School Musical.

23
01:15:08,000 --> 01:15:14,000
The film received generally mixed reviews from critics, with praise for its cast and music but
criticism for its sentimentality; it was more positively received by audiences.

24
01:18:24,000 --> 01:18:30,000
The film's soundtrack was commercially and critically
successful, reaching atop the U.S.

25
01:21:40,000 --> 01:21:46,000
Billboard 200, with its lead single, "Breaking
Free", reaching number four on the U.S.

26
01:24:56,000 --> 01:25:02,000
Billboard Hot 100.

27
01:28:12,000 --> 01:28:18,000
Two sequels, High School Musical 2 and High School Musical 3: Senior Year,
were released in August 2007 and October 2008 respectively.

28
01:31:28,000 --> 01:31:34,000
The film explores themes of high school, becoming an adult,
school performance, musical, basketball.

29
01:34:44,000 --> 01:34:50,000
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
